<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tinicum Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tinicum Island is an area on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> in Delaware County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. In 1643 Governor Johan Printz made it the seat of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, building Fort New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a> and his residence, the Printzhof, there; it was the first permanent European settlement in what became Pennsylvania. The site of the Printzhof is preserved in Governor Printz Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tinicum-island.jpg" alt="Tinicum Island" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rcsprinter123 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delaware_County_Pennsylvania_incorporated_and_unincorporated_areas_Tinicum_highlighted.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
